[1.636,"o","The metals, from nothing but citations.\r\n\r\nFive spheres, four of them metal, and no colour in any of their\r\ndescriptions. Normal-incidence reflectance integrated against the CIE\r\n1931 observer under D65.\r\n\r\n                      x         y         R         G         B        edge\r\n"]
[1.641,"o","  gold          0.38177   0.38870    1.0000    0.7020    0.3514    2.380 eV  \r\n"]
[1.646,"o","  copper        0.35575   0.34559    1.0000    0.6683    0.5606    2.129 eV  \r\n"]
[1.648,"o","  silver        0.31350   0.32967    1.0000    0.9945    0.9883    3.740 eV  \r\n"]
[1.65,"o","  aluminium     0.31161   0.32825    0.9837    0.9930    1.0000    1.342 eV  \r\n\r\n  D65 white is x 0.31271 y 0.32901, so gold sits 0.0913 away from it and\r\n  aluminium 0.0013 — a factor of 68, and nobody typed either.\r\n\r\n  The edge column is the one figure here that can be checked against\r\n  something other than the table this program read. It is where the\r\n  reflectance falls off a cliff, and that cliff is an interband\r\n  transition whose energy is a property of the metal's band structure:\r\n  gold's d-band threshold is quoted at about 2.4 eV, copper's at about\r\n  2.1, and silver's plasma edge at about 3.8 — which is why silver is\r\n  neutral across the visible and the other two are not.\r\n\r\n  Silver is 0.00103 from white and aluminium 0.00133, which makes aluminium\r\n  the control: it is nearly neutral, so a tint on it would be the\r\n  pipeline's rather than the metal's.\r\n\r\n  Gold, against the constant every renderer types:\r\n\r\n      derived      1.0000   0.7020   0.3514\r\n      typed        1.0000   0.7660   0.3360\r\n      difference   0.0000  -0.0640   0.0154\r\n\r\n  Within a few hundredths, from a direction nobody aimed. The\r\n  remaining difference is not error: the typed constant is somebody\r\n  else's table, under somebody else's illuminant, normalised somebody\r\n  else's way, and it has been copied between renderers for thirty\r\n  years without a citation. This one has two.\r\n\r\n  Which is the point. Swap gold's table for copper's and the same\r\n  code returns 1.0000 0.6683 0.5606 instead, because of physics rather than\r\n  because somebody typed pink.\r\n\r\nEvery colour above came out of a measurement.\r\n"]
