[14.429,"o","cornell — inspection sheet\r\n\r\nThe acceleration structure changes only the speed of the answer.\r\nEvery ray is intersected through the index and against every\r\nprimitive, and the two distances are compared exactly.\r\n\r\n"]
[14.433,"o","  (the box builds 25 nodes, so the two routes really differ)\r\n\r\n"]
[14.838,"o","  random origins and directions                    1000000 rays   agree\r\n"]
[14.903,"o","  exactly axis-aligned, on a grid of origins        242406 rays   agree\r\n"]
[15.002,"o","  starting exactly on the floor                     200000 rays   agree\r\n"]
[15.926,"o","  a tree built to be as deep as it can get          200000 rays   agree   (40 ties on coincident geometry)\r\n"]
[15.93,"o","  every coincident primitive is still in the tree      5000 of 5000   reachable\r\n"]
[16.043,"o","  with degenerate and zero-area triangles           200000 rays   agree\r\n\r\nRadiance does not depend on how big the room is.\r\n\r\n"]
[16.049,"o","  the same room at 1024x, compared exactly            4096 pixels   agree\r\n"]
[16.051,"o","  the same room at 0.000976562x, compared exactly      4096 pixels   agree\r\n"]
[16.057,"o","  the same room at 65536x, compared exactly           4096 pixels   agree\r\n"]
[16.061,"o","  the same room at 1.52588e-05x, compared exactly      4096 pixels   agree\r\n\r\nThe thread count changes the speed and not the answer.\r\n\r\n"]
[16.139,"o","  1 thread against 2, compared exactly                9216 pixels   agree\r\n"]
[16.159,"o","  1 thread against 3, compared exactly                9216 pixels   agree\r\n"]
[16.174,"o","  1 thread against 7, compared exactly                9216 pixels   agree\r\n"]
[16.188,"o","  1 thread against 16, compared exactly               9216 pixels   agree\r\n\r\nEvery sampler in the project agrees with the density it claims.\r\n\r\n"]
[16.25,"o","  lambert's sampler, four angles                   1048576 draws   agree   worst p = 0.020\r\n"]
[16.262,"o","  and the three alternatives draw identically       262144 draws   agree   0 differ\r\n"]
[16.363,"o","  two deliberate liars, which must be caught       2097152 draws   caught   p = 0.0e+00, 5.8e-05\r\n"]
[16.364,"o","  a mirror, whose density is a delta                   n/a        skipped, and says so\r\n\r\nA Lambertian of reflectance 1 vanishes in a uniform environment.\r\n\r\n"]
[16.38,"o","  reflectance 1.00, which must leave nothing          2348 pixels   agree   |L-1| = 0.000e+00\r\n"]
[16.396,"o","  reflectance 0.50, which must leave a disc           2348 pixels   agree   |L-1| = 5.000e-01\r\n"]
[16.408,"o","  reflectance 0.00, which must leave a disc           2348 pixels   agree   |L-1| = 1.000e+00\r\n"]
[16.423,"o","  a mirror of reflectance 1.00, which must leave nothing      2348 pixels   agree   |L-1| = 0.000e+00\r\n"]
[16.437,"o","  a mirror of reflectance 0.50, which must leave a disc      2348 pixels   agree   |L-1| = 5.000e-01\r\n"]
[16.451,"o","  a mirror of reflectance 0.25, which must leave a disc      2348 pixels   agree   |L-1| = 7.500e-01\r\n"]
[16.453,"o","  its BRDF integrates against the cosine to 1        65536 cells    agree   worst 2.398e-14\r\n\r\nEvery density in the project integrates to one over its own domain.\r\n\r\n"]
[16.458,"o","  lambert, grey albedo, over the sphere            1048576 cells    agree   +2.880e-14\r\n"]
[16.465,"o","  lambert, D65 as an albedo, over the sphere       1048576 cells    agree   +2.880e-14\r\n"]
[16.474,"o","  lambert, Cornell's red, over the sphere          1048576 cells    agree   +2.880e-14\r\n"]
[16.475,"o","  warp.hpp's cosine hemisphere, over the hemisphere   1048576 cells    agree   +1.244e-13\r\n  the hero wavelength draw, over 360-830 nm           1024 cells    agree   +1.728e-14\r\n  a density that is half of one, which must fail   1048576 cells    caught   -5.000e-01\r\n  a mirror, which integrates to nothing and should   1048576 cells    delta   +0.000e+00\r\n\r\n  The last row is not a failure. `bsdf.hpp`'s convention has a delta\r\n  lobe return zero from `pdf`, so a quadrature of it is zero — which\r\n  is the correct answer to the wrong question, and the reason the\r\n  convention returns zero rather than something plausible.\r\n\r\n  The residuals are summation rather than quadrature: every integrand\r\n  here is exactly integrated by a midpoint rule, so what is left is a\r\n  million doubles added end to end. The tolerance is 1e-12 and the\r\n  largest is 1.2e-13.\r\n\r\n  There is no light-sampling density yet, so there is no row for the\r\n  surface of a lamp. It arrives with v0.8, and so does this row.\r\n\r\nThe microfacet distribution covers the surface it models.\r\n\r\n                         with (n.m)     at h/2       without\r\n"]
[16.484,"o","  alpha = 0.05           +3.142e-04       4.00        1.0095   agree\r\n"]
[16.493,"o","  alpha = 0.10           +7.847e-05       4.00        1.0302   agree\r\n"]
[16.504,"o","  alpha = 0.25           +1.260e-05       4.00        1.1332   agree\r\n"]
[16.517,"o","  alpha = 0.50           +3.334e-06       4.00        1.3802   agree\r\n"]
[16.525,"o","  alpha = 1.00           +1.569e-06       4.00        2.0000   agree\r\n  alpha = 1 is 1/pi                                            identical\r\n"]
[16.526,"o","  normalised to dw       -5.000e-01                   1.0000   caught\r\n\r\n  The last row is a density over solid angle, which is what this\r\n  distribution is usually mistaken for: it integrates to exactly 1\r\n  in the last column, where every correct row above does not, and\r\n  it covers half the surface it claims to. A renderer with that\r\n  mistake in it is out by a factor of two on a rough material and\r\n  by almost nothing on a polished one, which is why it survives\r\n  being looked at.\r\n\r\n  There is no masking term yet, so nothing here is a BRDF and the\r\n  furnace has nothing to say about it. `smith.hpp` is item 0083 and\r\n  the furnace row for a rough conductor is item 0085.\r\n\r\nThe masking function follows from the distribution, and a borrowed\r\none does not fit.\r\n\r\n                               Lambda  vs its sum    covering   at h/2\r\n"]
[16.535,"o","  alpha 0.30, 15 deg      0.001612826   -3.47e-08   +8.44e-06     4.00   agree\r\n"]
[16.544,"o","  alpha 0.30, 45 deg      0.022015325   -2.46e-08   +6.06e-06     4.00   agree\r\n"]
[16.553,"o","  alpha 0.30, 75 deg      0.250589483   -1.09e-07   +1.80e-06     3.94   agree\r\n"]
[16.562,"o","  alpha 1.00, 15 deg      0.017638090   -3.86e-07   +1.12e-06     3.97   agree\r\n"]
[16.571,"o","  alpha 1.00, 45 deg      0.207106781   -3.33e-07   +7.24e-07     4.17   agree\r\n"]
[16.581,"o","  alpha 1.00, 75 deg      1.431851653   -4.11e-07   +1.23e-07     3.86   agree\r\n\r\n  The Lambda column is the closed form; it agrees with the sum it\r\n  was derived from to better than the quadrature's own error, and\r\n  the covering column is what the derivation required. Both fall\r\n  by four when the spacing is halved, so what is left in either is\r\n  the quadrature rather than the model.\r\n\r\n                             estimate       error             std errs\r\n"]
[16.628,"o","  furnace 0.30, 30 deg     0.99853792   -1.46e-03                 1.11   agree\r\n"]
[16.699,"o","  furnace 0.30, 85 deg     0.99826932   -1.73e-03                 0.76   agree\r\n"]
[16.744,"o","  furnace 1.00, 30 deg     1.00028086   +2.81e-04                 1.52   agree\r\n"]
[16.816,"o","  furnace 1.00, 85 deg     0.99962944   -3.71e-04                 0.59   agree\r\n\r\n                                                     covering         \r\n"]
[16.818,"o","  no masking at all                                 +3.71e-01            caught\r\n  Beckmann's, against D                             +1.28e-01            caught\r\n\r\n                           correlated   separable                ratio\r\n  alpha 0.10, 85 deg         0.658456    0.630530                1.044\r\n  alpha 0.50, 85 deg         0.172359    0.086458                1.994\r\n  alpha 1.00, 70 deg         0.342020    0.259804                1.316\r\n  alpha 1.00, 85 deg         0.087156    0.025708                3.390\r\n"]
[16.818,"o","  correlated >= separable  32400 pairs     closest     3.6e-14            holds\r\n\r\n  The last block is not a pass or a fail. The separable form is a\r\n  real model that real renderers ship, and what the sheet can say\r\n  about it is how much light it throws away at the edge of a rough\r\n  object — a factor of 3.4 at alpha 1 and 85 degrees — and that it\r\n  is never the larger of the two, which is a consequence of the\r\n  correlation being positive and is checked rather than asserted.\r\n\r\nA rough conductor of reflectance 1 vanishes, at every roughness.\r\n\r\n                                       walk       single   recovered\r\n"]
[16.872,"o","  alpha 0.1,  0 degrees        1.0000000000     0.988177    0.011823   vanished\r\n"]
[16.937,"o","  alpha 0.1, 75 degrees        1.0000000000     0.924373    0.075627   vanished\r\n"]
[17.009,"o","  alpha 0.4,  0 degrees        1.0000000000     0.786461    0.213539   vanished\r\n"]
[17.083,"o","  alpha 0.4, 75 degrees        1.0000000000     0.793717    0.206283   vanished\r\n"]
[17.264,"o","  alpha 1.0,  0 degrees        1.0000000000     0.307075    0.692925   vanished\r\n"]
[17.381,"o","  alpha 1.0, 75 degrees        1.0000000000     0.590591    0.409409   vanished\r\n"]
[17.415,"o","  the same, light not followed 0.3070746559                            caught\r\n\r\n  Compared with `==`, which an estimate rarely earns. At reflectance\r\n  1 nothing is absorbed at any facet, so the weight never changes and\r\n  every walk returns the same number; the mean of a constant is that\r\n  constant, exactly. The recovered column is the light item 0086\r\n  measured as lost.\r\n\r\n  `./cornell furnace --bsdf walk --table` splits it by scattering\r\n  order, which is the check with teeth: energy alone cannot see a walk\r\n  that conserves it while going the wrong way.\r\n\r\nEvery BSDF returns the same value with its arguments swapped.\r\n\r\n"]
[17.474,"o","  lambert, grey albedo                             1048576 pairs   agree   (524901 across the surface)\r\n"]
[17.536,"o","  lambert, D65 as an albedo                        1048576 pairs   agree   (524901 across the surface)\r\n"]
[17.598,"o","  lambert, Cornell's red                           1048576 pairs   agree   (524901 across the surface)\r\n"]
[17.621,"o","  one that weights only wi, which must fail        1048576 pairs   caught   worst 1.588e-01\r\n\r\n  Compared exactly. Refraction is the legitimate exception and does\r\n  not exist yet: crossing into a medium of different index multiplies\r\n  radiance by the squared index ratio, so what stays true there is\r\n  f(wo,wi)/n_o^2 == f(wi,wo)/n_i^2. v0.9 teaches this check that,\r\n  rather than relaxing it when it starts failing.\r\n\r\nNo non-finite value reaches the film.\r\n\r\n"]
[17.637,"o","  the box, every bin and every tristimulus          460800 values   all finite\r\n"]
[17.638,"o","  a NaN and an infinity, which must both fail            3 values   caught\r\n"]
[17.638,"o","  a camera with no idea which way is up                  1 path     black, not NaN\r\n"]
[17.644,"o","\r\n  The last row is a correction. `camera.hpp` says a degenerate `up`\r\n  hint produces a NaN direction left to propagate rather than be\r\n  papered over, and it does — into the ray. It stops there. Every\r\n  comparison against a NaN is false, so the intersection finds\r\n  nothing, the path escapes, and what arrives at the film is a\r\n  perfectly finite zero. The failure is a black image rather than a\r\n"]
[17.644,"o","  poisoned one, and no assertion at the film can catch it.\r\n"]
[17.645,"o","\r\nNo instrument catches everything, and none of them is redundant.\r\n"]
[17.645,"o","\r\n"]
[17.645,"o","  deliberately wrong model            furnace     chi2  density  swapped\r\n"]
[17.691,"o","  claims a flat density                     -   caught        -        -\r\n"]
[17.745,"o","  claims one 2% too steep                   -   caught        -        -\r\n"]
[17.795,"o","  a density that is half of one             -   caught   caught        -\r\n"]
[17.845,"o","  weights only the incoming ray        caught        -        -   caught\r\n"]
[17.846,"o","\r\n  Four models, each wrong in one way, none caught by every column.\r\n  The furnace and the swap each catch something nothing else does,\r\n  and the chi-squared catches two the furnace cannot see at all: a\r\n  density can be wrong by two percent and conserve energy exactly.\r\n\r\n  The density column is the honest exception, and it is worth saying\r\n  rather than arranging a fifth liar to justify it. Nothing here is\r\n  caught by it alone. A sampler that draws from a normalised\r\n  distribution and reports an unnormalised density is the row above,\r\n  and the chi-squared sees it because this implementation compares\r\n  against the density times the draw count rather than renormalising\r\n  to the observed total — so a missing factor of two is a chi2/dof of\r\n  515 rather than a perfect fit. A density that is unnormalised and\r\n  whose sampler agrees with it cannot exist, because the missing half\r\n  has to go somewhere.\r\n\r\n  What the column is for arrives in v0.8. Multiple importance\r\n  sampling evaluates one strategy's density on another "]
[17.847,"o","strategy's\r\n"]
[17.848,"o","  directions, which is a use of a pdf with no sampler of its own\r\n"]
[17.848,"o","  attached — and there a chi-squared has nothing to compare. It is\r\n"]
[17.849,"o","  also exact where the chi-squared is statistical: -5.000e-01 against\r\n"]
[17.849,"o","  a p-value.\r\n"]
[17.849,"o","\r\nFresnel, and the three things nobody wrote.\r\n"]
[17.85,"o","\r\n"]
[17.854,"o","  Brewster at n = 1.330: searched 53.0612, arctan 53.0612    agree\r\n"]
[17.863,"o","  Brewster at n = 1.500: searched 56.3099, arctan 56.3099    agree\r\n"]
[17.872,"o","  Brewster at n = 2.417: searched 67.5234, arctan 67.5234    agree\r\n"]
[17.873,"o","      water, window glass, diamond. The middle one is 56.31 degrees\r\n      and is why polarising sunglasses work on a wet road.\r\n"]
[17.876,"o","\r\n"]
[17.876,"o","  past the critical angle, glass to air, R is 1                  48140 angles   agree   100% exact, worst 0.0 ulp\r\n"]
[17.876,"o","  at normal incidence the general form meets the closed one          6 indices  agree   worst 1.1e-16\r\n"]
[17.881,"o","  no reflectance exceeds 1, arriving through anything clear     810009 pairs    agree   largest 1\r\n"]
[17.882,"o","  from 80 to 90 degrees both polarisations rise to 1             glass        agree   R = 0.99989\r\n"]
[17.901,"o","  a conductor's r_p bottoms at 70.02 degrees, not zero       n=0.2 k=3        agree   R = 0.86497\r\n"]
[17.902,"o","\r\n  The fit next door, against the law. Unpolarised, 90,001 angles:\r\n\r\n    index                         R0   max error  mean error   worst at\r\n"]
[17.902,"o","    water,        1.330      0.02006     0.05992     0.01086    83.8 deg  \r\n"]
[17.903,"o","    window glass, 1.500      0.04000     0.03569     0.00922    85.0 deg  \r\n"]
[17.904,"o","    diamond,      2.417      0.17197     0.07544     0.01338    84.5 deg  \r\n"]
[17.905,"o","    n=0.2  k=3.0             0.92337     0.01544     0.00461    76.5 deg  \r\n"]
[17.909,"o","    n=1.1  k=7.0             0.91762     0.10112     0.02121    83.4 deg  \r\n"]
[17.91,"o","    n=0.05 k=4.2             0.98933     0.00604     0.00157    79.6 deg  \r\n"]
[17.91,"o","\r\n    About one percent on average and three to eight at the worst,\r\n    from one line, which is why it won. Every maximum is at 84 or\r\n    85 degrees: the fit is pinned at both ends by construction and\r\n    the last few degrees before grazing are where it has the most\r\n    room to be wrong.\r\n\r\n    The complex rows are the ones worth reading. The error is not\r\n    a multiple of the dielectric error — it is 0.006 at one index\r\n    and 0.101 at another, better and worse than glass — because a\r\n    conductor's curve is not the shape Schlick fitted and how badly\r\n"]
[17.91,"o","    that shows depends on the index. There is no factor to apply.\r\n"]
[17.91,"o","\r\n  What averaging the two polarisations costs. Unpolarised light,\r\n  two bounces off glass, same plane of incidence:\r\n\r\n"]
[17.91,"o","       first   second       tracked      averaged    ratio\r\n"]
[17.91,"o","        0.00     0.00    0.00160000    0.00160000   1.0000\r\n"]
[17.911,"o","       45.00    45.00    0.00426907    0.00252405   0.5912\r\n"]
[17.911,"o","       56.31    56.31    0.01094149    0.00547075   0.5000\r\n"]
[17.912,"o","       70.00    70.00    0.04578120    0.02925555   0.6390\r\n"]
[17.913,"o","       85.00    85.00    0.38981461    0.37552341   0.9633\r\n"]
[17.913,"o","\r\n  the worst of those is exactly one half                     at Brewster        agree   0.500000\r\n"]
[17.914,"o","  turn the second surface ninety degrees and it is unbounded   crossed        agree   0.00547075 vs 0\r\n"]
[17.914,"o","\r\n  Two crossed polarisers, the oldest demonstration in optics. The\r\n  truth is that nothing comes back; this renderer predicts half a\r\n  percent of it. What it cannot produce is the *absence* of a\r\n  reflection, and no factor corrects for that — it needs four\r\n  Stokes parameters per wavelength and a Mueller matrix at every\r\n"]
[17.915,"o","  interaction, which is item 0143 and is under `later`.\r\n"]
[17.915,"o","\r\n  The last row is why the dielectric and the conductor are one\r\n  function here. Brewster's angle exists because `r_p` can reach\r\n  zero; with a complex index it cannot, and nothing had to be\r\n"]
[17.915,"o","  written for that — the same equation stops having a root.\r\n"]
[17.916,"o","\r\nA metal's colour comes out of its table, and lands where the band\r\nstructure says it should.\r\n\r\n"]
[17.916,"o","                      x        y from white    edge, eV  published\r\n"]
[17.917,"o","  gold          0.38177  0.38870    0.09128       2.380        2.4   agree\r\n"]
[17.917,"o","  copper        0.35575  0.34559    0.04612       2.129        2.1   agree\r\n"]
[17.917,"o","  silver        0.31350  0.32967    0.00103       3.740        3.8   agree\r\n"]
[17.918,"o","  aluminium     0.31161  0.32825    0.00133       1.342        1.5   agree\r\n"]
[17.919,"o","\r\n      Four metals, four band-structure energies, none of them typed\r\n      into this program and none of them derivable from the tables\r\n"]
[17.92,"o","      without the equations in between.\r\n"]
[17.92,"o","\r\n  derived gold against the constant everyone types 1 .766 .336        agree   worst 0.0640\r\n"]
[17.921,"o","      derived 1.0000 0.7020 0.3514, from two columns of measured n and k\r\n"]
[17.921,"o","      through Fresnel through the 1931 observer, with nothing typed.\r\n      The gap is not error: the constant is somebody else's table,\r\n"]
[17.921,"o","      under somebody else's illuminant, copied between renderers for\r\n      thirty years without a citation.\r\n"]
[17.922,"o","\r\n  And the criterion that is not a measurement: no metal in this\r\n  project has an RGB value in any file. CI greps for the constant\r\n  and fails the build if it appears outside the one array that\r\n"]
[17.922,"o","  exists to be compared against.\r\n"]
[17.923,"o","\r\nEvery claim above holds.\r\n"]
[17.923,"o","3.5 s. There is no --quick, because there is nothing worth skipping.\r\n"]
